✅ What is JIT (Just-In-Time Compiler)?
JIT is part of the JVM Execution Engine.
It compiles “hot” bytecode → native machine code at runtime, so that code runs faster next times.

Without JIT: JVM mostly interprets bytecode (slower)
With JIT: frequently used methods become native → much faster


🔹 Why “Just-In-Time”?
Compilation happens during program execution (not ahead like javac).
javac  → .java to bytecode   (before run)
JIT    → bytecode to native  (while running, for hot paths)


🧠 How JIT Works (flow)

1) Class loaded, methods start running
2) Interpreter executes bytecode initially
3) JVM profiles: counts how often methods/loops run
4) When a method becomes “hot” → JIT compiles it to native code
5) Later calls jump to that native code (fast)
6) Optionally re-optimizes / de-optimizes if assumptions break


🔁 Full pipeline

.java
  ↓ javac
.class (bytecode)     ← platform-independent
  ↓
JVM Execution Engine
  ├─ Interpreter     → start fast, run slower
  └─ JIT Compiler    → compile hot code → native machine code
         ↓
   CPU runs native instructions (platform-dependent)


🔹 Interpreter vs JIT

|                 | Interpreter              | JIT                              |
| --------------- | ------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
| When            | Immediately              | After code is hot                |
| Output          | Executes bytecode step by step | Native machine code         |
| Startup         | Faster startup           | Extra compile cost               |
| Peak speed      | Slower                   | Much faster for hot paths        |
| Best for        | Cold / rarely used code  | Loops, frequent methods          |


🔹 HotSpot compilers (common interview detail)
HotSpot JVM often uses tiered compilation:

- C1 (Client compiler) → faster compile, lighter optimizations (quick warmup)
- C2 (Server compiler) → slower compile, heavier optimizations (peak performance)

Typical path: interpret → C1 → C2 for very hot methods


🔹 What makes code “hot”?
- Method called many times
- Loop iterated many times
JVM uses invocation/back-edge counters (exact thresholds are JVM-internal / tunable)


🔹 Optimizations JIT may do (high level)
- Inlining (replace method call with body)
- Dead code elimination
- Loop optimizations
- Escape analysis (sometimes stack-allocate / scalar replace)
- Speculative opts based on observed types (can de-optimize if wrong later)


⚡ Important interview points
1) JIT ≠ javac
   javac = source → bytecode
   JIT  = bytecode → native (at runtime)

2) Bytecode portable; JIT output is NOT
   Native code is for that OS/CPU — that’s why each platform needs its own JVM

3) Warmup matter
   Long-running servers benefit most (JIT has time to optimize)
   Short CLI tools may stay mostly interpreted

4) You don’t call JIT yourself
   It’s automatic inside the JVM


🧪 How to “see” related behavior (optional)
- javap -c MyClass          → view bytecode (not JIT native)
- JVM flags exist for JIT logging (e.g. PrintCompilation) — interview-level awareness enough


💬 Common Q&A

Q: Does JVM always use JIT?
A: HotSpot can use interpreter + JIT. Cold code may never be JIT-compiled.

Q: Is JIT mandatory in Java spec?
A: JVM must execute bytecode; JIT is an implementation performance feature (HotSpot has it).

Q: Why is Java sometimes slow at start then faster?
A: Warmup — interpreter first, then JIT optimizes hot paths.


🧠 Interview one-liner
“JIT compiles frequently executed bytecode into native machine code at runtime, improving peak performance after warmup while keeping Java portable via bytecode.”
